  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 313 AM CDT Tue Aug 11 2026\n\nThe wavy west-to-east quasi-stationary front referenced in \nprevious discussions sill remains draped over WY-NE-IA-IL-IN \nthis morning, with low pressure centers along it analyzed over \nthe NE/IA border and the western SD/NE border. This appears to \nbe a bit farther south than earlier forecasts, which as such is \nkeeping the bulk of thunderstorm activity shunted to the south, \nmainly over far southeastern SD, northeastern NE and into \nnorthwestern IA. That said, the expectation is that this front \nand the low over the NE/IA border will slide slightly northeast,\nwhich still may interact with strong H85-H7 winds (in the \n40-60kt range) and a rather unstable environment featuring \ndewpoints still into the mid 60s to lower 70s and PWATs over \n1.5\". Weak isentropic lift north of the front has kept scattered\nlight rain showers sliding eastward over the WFO MPX coverage \narea overnight, only producing a trace to a couple hundredths at\nany given location. However, the longer we go through the early\nmorning hours and past sunrise, the lesser the chance that any \norganized convection will impact southwestern and southern \nportions of our coverage area. Still kept \"likely\" PoPs in place\nfor far southern portions of our coverage area but confidence \nis dropping hour-by-hour that this may come to fruition so some \nchanges may still be needed in the gridded hourly forecasts \nthrough sunrise.\n\nOnce past sunrise, upper level forcing in the form of a deep\ntrough over central NOAM will flatten and shift eastward, \nallowing for northwest flow to develop while the surface \nfeatures also shift off to the southeast. This will allow for \npartial clearing and a more stable atmosphere to take hold. The \nSWODY1 Marginal Risk is mainly for any morning activity and PoPs\naccordingly drop from late morning on with a mix of sun and \nclouds underneath passing high cirrus and smoke over the area \nfor this afternoon-evening. A few light rain showers are \npossible in western MN tonight in response to a weak low level \ntrough emanating from a surface low over NE/KS but anything that\ndoes develop tonight will be of nearly no consequence.\n\nWednesday during the day looks to remain dry but weak nocturnal \nforcing close to the same stalled out frontal boundary may again\nbring scattered showers/thunderstorms to southwestern and \nsouthern MN late Wednesday night into early Thursday morning. \nStrong-to- severe storms are possible due to another modest \nsurge in low-to-mid level shear, but coverage will be meager and\noverall upper level forcing is lacking so there is a much \nbetter chance of not having strong-to-severe storms than there \nis in them being realized.\n\nA small airmass change looks to occur starting on Thursday as \nnorthwest flow takes a better hold over the region and modified \ncolder Canadian high pressure makes inroads from the northwest. \nThis will start a period of highs dropping into the 70s area-\nwide and lows in the 50s outside of the Twin Cities metro (near \n60 within). There is better agreement on an organized area of \nlow pressure arriving from the southwest Friday into Saturday, \ndragging a slug of moisture from the Deep South into the Upper \nMidwest as it arrives. This looks to result in fairly widespread\nrainfall from late Friday into much of Saturday, including QPFs\nover an inch for much of southern MN over a 30-36 hour period. \nNot expecting much in the way of thunderstorm activity but a few\nembedded t-storms are certainly possible. \n\nA slight warming trend is now noted in the blended model\nconsensus for the first part of next week, in conjunction with\na drying out period as well.",
